{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"6a23772d-b595-408e-9029-24ab809d8905","status":"SUCCESS","response":"{\"findings\":[{\"assessment\":\"sound\",\"recommendation\":\"The strict type check 'type(x) is not int' correctly excludes JSON booleans and floats, which is essential because bool is an int subclass in Python. The test battery should explicitly assert that inputs containing True or False as endpoints trigger exit 2 with empty stdout.\",\"topic\":\"type_validation\"},{\"assessment\":\"sound\",\"recommendation\":\"Reading MAX_BYTES + 1 into memory cleanly enforces the 131072-byte stdin limit and atomicity. The test suite should test exact boundary sizes (131072 bytes valid vs 131073 bytes invalid), as well as multi-request CRLF and lone LF line endings.\",\"topic\":\"framing_and_boundaries\"},{\"assessment\":\"sound\",\"recommendation\":\"Criteria C3 proposes an independent set-based oracle for small intervals. When implementing property-based tests in test_range_audit.py, ensure the range generation for the oracle remains within a small coordinate span to avoid excessive memory allocation during the test run.\",\"topic\":\"oracle_strategy\"}],\"reason\":\"The implementation range_audit.py and README.md adhere strictly to the functional contract, including UTF-8 NDJSON parsing, interval normalization, strict integer type enforcement, atomic batch error handling, and limits. The criteria document establishes a solid verification plan before sealing test_range_audit.py.\",\"schema\":1,\"tests_executed\":false,\"verdict\":\"accept\"}\n","duration_seconds":110.868690112,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":24643,"output_tokens":15836,"thinking_tokens":15533,"cache_read_tokens":0,"total_tokens":40479}}}
